[{"id":84907,"sender_id":842,"sender_full_name":"Zhen Huan","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/c24a7269ec5dbfcdb95099be6f0ae240?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Classification.20of.20coherent.202-group.20representation/near/84907\">#1</a> As far as I know, we don't have a classification of ALL 2-group representation in nontrivial case. The only relevant work I know is the paper \"Central Extension of Smooth 2-groups and a finite-dimensional String 2-group\" by Schommer-Pries. In it the central extensions of smooth 2-groups are classified in term of Segal-Mitchison topological group cohomology.<br>Is there any results in this direction?</p>","timestamp":1596555407,"subject":"Classification of coherent 2-group representation","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":84908,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Classification.20of.20coherent.202-group.20representation/near/84908\">#2</a> (It seems you don’t mean to say “representations” but “central extensions”? There is a close relation, but the general topic of (“linear”) representations of higher groups is deep and murky, while the central extensions of higher groups are much better understood.)</p>\n<p>Generally, extensions of an <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∞</span></span></span></span>-group <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span></span></span></span> by a braided <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∞</span></span></span></span>-group <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span> (both can be the coherent 2-groups that you are after, but may also be more general) are equivalently <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">B</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{B}A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbf\">B</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span>-principal bundles over <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">B</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{B}G</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbf\">B</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span></span></span></span>. </p>\n<p>(this is Section 4.3 in NSS12 <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1207.0248.pdf#page=38\">here</a>, or Section 3.6.14 in dcct <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1310.7930v1.pdf#page=341\">here</a>)</p>\n<p>By the general classification of principal <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∞</span></span></span></span>-bundles, (Theorem 3.17 in NSS12 <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1207.0248.pdf#page=25\">here</a>) this means that these extensions are classified by maps</p>\n<p><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">B</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>⟶</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n   \\mathbf{B}G \\longrightarrow \\mathbf{B}^2 A\n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6971em;vertical-align:-0.011em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbf\">B</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⟶</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbf\">B</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span></span></p>\n<p>hence by</p>\n<p><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">B</mi><mi>G</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n  \\pi_0 \\mathbf{H}(\\mathbf{B}G, \\mathbf{B}^2 A)\n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1141em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">π</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0359em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord mathbf\">H</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathbf\">B</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbf\">B</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span></span></p>\n<p>hence by</p>\n<p><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n  H^2_{\\mathrm{Grp}}(G, A)\n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.2472em;vertical-align:-0.3831em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0813em;\">H</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8641em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.453em;margin-left:-0.0813em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathrm mtight\">Grp</span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.113em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3831em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span></span></p>\n<p>the “cohesive <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∞</span></span></span></span>-group degree-2 cohomology of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span></span></span></span> with coeffcients in <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span>”. Again, you may restrict this to coherent 2-groups if desired.</p>\n<p>This holds for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span></span></span></span> being group <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∞</span></span></span></span>-stacks, but to compute classifications you’ll want to restrict to controlled cases where, say, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span></span></span></span> is a Lie group.</p>\n<p>In the case that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">G</span></span></span></span> is a Lie group, the above reduces to Segal-Brylinski smooth group cohomology\n(Theorem 4.4.36 in dcct <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1310.7930v1.pdf#page=488\">here</a>),</p>\n<p>Combining all this, one finds in particular the String 2-group extensions (Section 5.1.4 in dcct <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1310.7930v1.pdf#page=583\">here</a> )</p>","timestamp":1596558216,"subject":"Classification of coherent 2-group representation","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":84910,"sender_id":842,"sender_full_name":"Zhen Huan","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/c24a7269ec5dbfcdb95099be6f0ae240?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Classification.20of.20coherent.202-group.20representation/near/84910\">#3</a> Thanks a lot. I realize my question led to misunderstanding. What I'm curious about is the classification of the representations of a specific 2-group E which is a central extenstion of G by A. I will read the paper first.</p>","timestamp":1596584149,"subject":"Classification of coherent 2-group representation","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":84912,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Classification.20of.20coherent.202-group.20representation/near/84912\">#4</a> The only systematic way, that I am aware of, to get the interesting 2-representations of 2-groups remains that in: </p>\n<p>Appendix A “2-Vector spaces and the canonical 2-representation” of\n<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.1079\">arXiv:0806.1079</a></p>\n<p>As formulated there  this invokes strict 2-group models of the given 2-group. This should be just a technical convenience, not a restriction. For the string 2-groups the strict 2-group model is established in <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0504123\">arXiv:math/0504123</a>.</p>","timestamp":1596596115,"subject":"Classification of coherent 2-group representation","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":84913,"sender_id":842,"sender_full_name":"Zhen Huan","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/c24a7269ec5dbfcdb95099be6f0ae240?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/Classification.20of.20coherent.202-group.20representation/near/84913\">#5</a> Thanks!</p>","timestamp":1596614990,"subject":"Classification of coherent 2-group representation","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]}]